<Guide slug="four-week-revision-plan" description="A simple 4-week revision plan for the Life in the UK test, with what to study each week and when to start mock exams.">
  <p>
    Four weeks is enough time to prepare well if you study a little every day. This plan assumes about 20 minutes a
    day. If you have less time, stretch it to five or six weeks.
  </p>

  <h2>Week 1: values, the UK, and modern society</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Read chapters 1 and 2 of the handbook. They are short.</li>
    <li>Start chapter 4: religion, festivals, patron saints and capital cities.</li>
    <li>Answer practice questions on each section the same day you read it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Week 2: history</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Read chapter 3, the longest, in four parts: early Britain, the Middle Ages, the Tudors and Stuarts, and from 1700 to today.</li>
    <li>Keep a list of dates and test yourself on them daily. See <a href="/guides/key-dates-to-learn">key dates to learn</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Week 3: government, law, and the rest of chapter 4</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Read chapter 5: Parliament, elections, courts and your role in the community.</li>
    <li>Finish chapter 4: sport, arts, literature and places of interest.</li>
    <li>Take your first timed mock exam at the end of the week.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Week 4: mocks and weak spots</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Take a timed mock every other day.</li>
    <li>After each mock, review every wrong answer and practise those sections again.</li>
    <li>Aim to score well above 18 out of 24, consistently, before your test.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="note">
    The Waymark app builds this plan for you around your actual test date, and brings back the facts you're about to
    forget. <a href="/app">Try it free</a>.
  </p>
</Guide>
